in this episode of cloud performance  atlas I helped some nursery experts with  their GCS upload performance will we  rock this problem to sleep stay tuned to  find out  bundle cam is a company that helps  provide peace of mind through durable  high performance nursery cameras their  setup was pretty simple  every half second the camera would snap  a photo and store it locally later a  batch of those photos would be uploaded  to Google Cloud storage  for display and analysis the problem was  that they were seeing that their upload  times for a batch of images was  painfully slow during the course of 20  minutes this slowdown would result in a  serious backlog of images that needed  uploading eventually resulting in the  camera running out of memory and  stopping working at first glance this  could be a cloud storage issue so let's  run down the list of things to test  firstly we ran perf diag on a sample  bucket that they created and verified  that it had a high throughput from  clients in the region this meant that  the bucket itself was performing  properly in terms of connection and  upload speed from the client so that  wasn't our problem next we checked that  they were using the fastest API as we've  shown the GS util API tends to be a tad  slower than the direct API counterpart  but that wasn't the problem either the  cameras were on embedded hardware so  they were forced to use the direct api's  rather than GS util and they were using  the parallel upload API so again no  problem there at this point I needed to  do some research myself so I turned to  Michael use 2017 next talk where he  provided details on how GCS works behind  the scenes the important bit for us goes  something like this  when uploading files to GCS the load  balancer will use file names to decide  how to distribute connections between  shards to handle upload as such the more  diverse the file names the better the  system can balance the load likewise the  more similar the file names the harder  it is to reach peak performance when  there's contention to GCS and that was  exactly our issue the developer was  using the timestamp in the file path  running right into this performance  problem the fix for bundle cam was  pretty simple for pending the hash of  the file name to the file name itself  which allowed GCS to better partition  the fixed range into shards for better  performance you can see the difference  between the linear file names and the  hashed ones pretty easily to learn more  about how bundle cam improved their  upload performance check out the Related  article linked in the description below  and don't forget to check out the rest  of our cloud performance Atlas content  because when it comes to performance  every millisecond counts
 

